NullPointerDereference: {This vulnerability occurs when code calls a method that may return a null value and subsequently attempts to access a member or invoke a method on that null object without first verifying whether the object is null. If the returned object is indeed null, dereferencing it leads to a runtime exception, typically resulting in application crashes or undefined behavior. The core issue stems from the assumption that a method's return value will always be non-null, without implementing safeguards such as null checks or alternative handling mechanisms. Ensuring that methods explicitly document whether null is a valid return or employing null-safe practices prevents dereferencing null references, thereby improving application robustness and stability.}

