// 3. Processing form data with unchecked parameters for display
import javax.servlet.*;
import javax.servlet.http.*;
import java.io.*;

public class ProfileServlet extends HttpServlet {
    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) throws ServletException, IOException {
        String bio = request.getParameter("bio");
        response.setContentType("text/html");
        PrintWriter out = response.getWriter();
        out.println("<h1>User Bio</h1>");
        // Unsafe output of user input (possible XSS)
        out.println("<p>" + bio + "</p>");
    }
}

metadata.report:
VULNERABILITY REPORT

1) Vulnerability Description:
The code is vulnerable to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks due to improper handling of user input. It directly outputs unescaped user-provided data (`bio`) into the HTML response, which attackers can exploit to inject malicious scripts that execute in other users' browsers.

2) Vulnerable Code:
```java
public class ProfileServlet extends HttpServlet {
    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) throws ServletException, IOException {
        String bio = request.getParameter("bio");
        response.setContentType("text/html");
        PrintWriter out = response.getWriter();
        out.println("<h1>User Bio</h1>");
        // Unsafe output of user input (possible XSS)
        out.println("<p>" + bio + "</p>");
    }
}
```

3) Root Cause:
The root cause is the lack of proper sanitization or encoding of user input before rendering it into the HTML page. The code directly outputs the raw input (`bio`) without escaping special HTML characters, making it susceptible to code injection if users include malicious scripts in their input.

4) Impact:
An attacker can inject malicious JavaScript code via the `bio` parameter. When other users view this profile page, the script executes within their browsers, potentially leading to session hijacking, theft of sensitive data, or defacement of the website.

5) Mitigation:
- Escape user input before displaying it in HTML using proper encoding libraries (e.g., JSTL `<c:out>` or Apache Commons StringEscapeUtils).
- Validate and sanitize input on the server side to prevent injection of malicious code.
- Implement Content Security Policy (CSP) headers to restrict executable scripts.
- Use frameworks or libraries that automatically handle output encoding.
- Avoid directly inserting user input into HTML content without sanitization.

