metadata.report:
Vulnerability Report
----------------------

1) Vulnerability Description:
The presented servlet directly incorporates unvalidated user input into HTML content without any sanitization or encoding. This practice exposes the application to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities, where malicious users can inject scripts or HTML tags that execute in the browser of other end-users, potentially leading to data theft, session hijacking, or other security breaches.

2) Vulnerable Code:
```java
// 7. Processing unchecked user input to generate HTML content
import javax.servlet.*;
import javax.servlet.http.*;
import java.io.*;

public class DynamicHtmlServlet extends HttpServlet {
    protected void doGet(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) throws ServletException, IOException {
        String userName = request.getParameter("name");
        response.setContentType("text/html");
        PrintWriter out = response.getWriter();
        // Outputting unchecked input directly
        out.println("<html><body>");
        out.println("<h2>Hello, " + userName + "</h2>");
        out.println("</body></html>");
    }
}
```

3) Root Cause:
The root cause of the vulnerability is the failure to sanitize or encode user-supplied input (`userName`) before embedding it into HTML output. Allowing raw input to be directly inserted into the webpage can enable attackers to craft inputs containing malicious scripts or HTML tags, which will then be executed or rendered by the browser.

4) Impact:
- Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks, leading to execution of malicious scripts in the context of legitimate users.
- Potential data theft, session hijacking, or defacement of the web page.
- Damage to user trust and potential legal or compliance implications.

5) Mitigation:
- Properly encode user input when inserting it into HTML, e.g., by escaping special characters (`<`, `>`, `&`, `"`, `'`) to prevent script execution.
- Use security libraries or frameworks that offer automatic output encoding.
- Validate and sanitize all user input at the earliest opportunity.
- Consider using templating engines that automatically handle HTML encoding.
- Implement Content Security Policy (CSP) headers to restrict execution of inline scripts.

By adopting these measures, the application can defend against XSS vulnerabilities caused by unsanitized user input.

